Left-justify with a negative * width in every awk printf conversion.
As in C, a negative width taken from a * argument means the - flag and
the absolute width, but awk_printf() dropped a negative width except
for %c and %s, so printf "%*d", -5, 3 printed "3" rather than "3    ".
Turn a negative * width into the - flag and its absolute value, which
also covers %c and %s.

---
 editors/awk.c |   10 ++++++----
 1 file changed, 6 insertions(+), 4 deletions(-)

--- a/editors/awk.c
+++ b/editors/awk.c
@@ -2666,6 +2666,11 @@
 		if (c == '*') {
 			width = (int)getvar_i(evaluate(nextarg(&n), TMPVAR));
 //bb_error_msg("*width=%d", width);
+			if (width < 0) {
+				/* as in C, a negative width left-justifies */
+				*baked_cur++ = '-';
+				width = -width;
+			}
 			c = *++fmt_cur;
 			//if (isdigit(c)) /* we do not support "%*2$d" format */
 			//	syntax_error("invalid format specifier");
@@ -2704,10 +2709,7 @@
